Moise theorem: every topological 3-manifold is triangulable, PL structure unique. DAG:
- M closed connected topological 3-manifold.
- Shalen/Moise: triangulation exists.
- From triangulation: M = finite union of tetrahedra glued along faces; barycentric subdivision; regular neighborhood theory; dual cell decomposition.
- Perelman's flow gives a *smooth* structure: the Ricci-flow-with-surgery yields M = #ᵢ S³/Γᵢ (smooth pieces); then triangulate each S³/Γᵢ via PL structure on S³/Γᵢ (e.g. from the round S³ triangulation ∂Δ⁴ + group action compatibility) — via "smooth ⟹ PL triangulable" (Whitehead/Cairns: every smooth manifold is triangulable) — hence M triangulable without Moise.
- So in the Perelman branch, Moise for *arbitrary* topological 3-manifolds is NOT needed if one starts with a smooth manifold: the smoothing branch: topological input M → (Moise) triangulated → smooth (every 3-manifold has a smooth structure via Moise + PL structure — Whitehead: PL manifolds of dim ≤ 3 are smoothable) → run Ricci flow.
- DAG nodes: (1) Triangulation of S³/Γᵢ via smooth structure (Cairns–Whitehead), (2) connected-sum of triangulations = triangulation, (3) gluing along spheres ∂D³ ≅ S² homeomorphism (MY THEOREM 3!), (4) finite connected sums, (5) recognition: π₁ trivial ⟹ each Γᵢ trivial (covering/van Kampen), (6) each piece ≅ S³ (sphere recognition via Rubinstein-Thompson or via Γᵢ=1), (7) M ≅ S³.

